/// \defgroup grp_w2_ww3d_core_helpers WW3D2 helpers: hierarchy trees, texture bit depths, Direct3D errors and small mesh, line and camera members
/// \ingroup grp_w3d_rendering
/// \brief The hierarchy tree's default setup and teardown, the texture
///        format bit-depth table, the fatal Direct3D error handler, one
///        asset-catalog registrar, and single members of the mesh, line
///        renderer, W3D shader description and camera classes.
///
/// Small, settled pieces of the W3D engine (WW3D2, \ref sub_w3d_rendering).
/// Each one is a retail function that the project reconstructs in its own
/// unit under Code/Libraries/Source/WWVegas/WW3D2/. Several are dedicated units that
/// keep one body apart from its class's main source file, so they compile
/// against a reduced view of the class. The declarations Doxygen reads come
/// from the BFME 1 reconstruction's WW3D2 headers
/// (reference/open-bfme-1/game/Libraries/Source/WWVegas/WW3D2/), or from
/// the BFME 2 unit itself where no header declares the function.
///
/// - HTreeClass: HTreeClass::Init_Default builds the one-pivot default
///   skeleton and HTreeClass::~HTreeClass releases a tree.
/// - Texture formats: Get_Bits_Per_Pixel gives the storage cost of a
///   texture format in bits per pixel.
/// - Direct3D errors: Log_DX8_ErrorCode is where a failed, checked Direct3D
///   call ends up. In BFME 2 it raises a fatal crash report.
/// - Asset catalog: Register_Aggregate_Prototype registers a catalog entry
///   under its name. Its name is in doubt; see its entry.
/// - Meshes: MeshModelClass::Compute_Ram_Size estimates a mesh model's
///   memory; MeshMatDescClass::Disable_Backface_Culling makes a mesh's
///   materials two-sided.
/// - Line rendering: SegLineRendererClass::operator= copies a line
///   renderer's settings.
/// - W3D file structures: W3d_Shader_Reset fills a W3dShaderStruct with the
///   format's default shader settings.
/// - Cameras: CameraClass::Set_Viewport sets the screen rectangle a camera
///   renders into.
///
/// \par Differences from Zero Hour
/// These bodies show several places where BFME 2's W3D engine differs from
/// Zero Hour's; some of the changes are already present in BFME 1. Each is
/// described on its entity:
/// - Hierarchy pivots store each transform as a rotation quaternion plus a
///   translation, carry an extra per-pivot scalar, and captured bones live
///   in a list owned by the tree (HTreeClass).
/// - The texture-format values that reach Get_Bits_Per_Pixel are Direct3D's
///   own D3DFORMAT codes, not Zero Hour's WW3DFormat numbering, and the
///   result is in bits, not bytes.
/// - A failed Direct3D call is fatal in the retail build (Log_DX8_ErrorCode).
/// - The cull-mode flag sits at a different bit of the packed shader word
///   (MeshMatDescClass::Disable_Backface_Culling).
/// - SegLineRendererClass has one extra field whose role is unknown.
///
/// \par Modding
/// - Skeletons are data. A tree's pivots come from the hierarchy chunks of a
///   W3D file, read by HTreeClass::Load_W3D. A model whose hierarchy name is
///   empty, or names a hierarchy that cannot be found, gets the one-pivot
///   default tree from HTreeClass::Init_Default instead of failing to load.
///   The warning for a missing hierarchy is a debug-build message, so retail
///   reports nothing.
/// - Asset names are registered once. The asset catalog reader, and the
///   registrar Register_Aggregate_Prototype, skip a name that is already
///   registered, so the first registration of a name wins.
/// - Two-sided rendering is a per-mesh flag in the W3D file. BFME 2's
///   MeshModelClass::Load_W3D sets it from the W3D_MESH_FLAG_TWO_SIDED
///   attribute of the mesh header, as Zero Hour's does, and the
///   MeshModelClass::post_process it calls at the end of loading then calls
///   MeshMatDescClass::Disable_Backface_Culling.
/// - A texture whose format is missing from Get_Bits_Per_Pixel's table
///   counts as zero bytes of pixel data in BFME 2's texture-memory estimate.
///
/// \par Remastering
/// - Log_DX8_ErrorCode is the Direct3D layer's failure policy for checked
///   calls, and in retail that policy is a fatal crash report. A port
///   that must survive device loss or failed resource creation replaces
///   this policy, not only the Direct3D calls.
/// - The texture format values that reach Get_Bits_Per_Pixel in BFME 2 are
///   Direct3D's D3DFORMAT codes; Open-BFME-1 reports the same for BFME 1's
///   whole texture pipeline. A renderer on another API needs a translation
///   at that boundary, and the bit depths in Get_Bits_Per_Pixel.
/// - A camera viewport is a fraction of the display, not pixels.
///   W3DView::setOrigin converts the view's pixel origin, and
///   W3DView::setWidth and W3DView::setHeight convert its far edge (origin
///   plus size, in pixels), to fractions of the display width and height.
///   Among the identified callers, W3DView::setWidth and W3DView::setHeight
///   also set the camera's aspect ratio (CameraClass::Set_Aspect_Ratio)
///   from the view's pixel size, and W3DView::setWidth, but not setHeight,
///   also sets the horizontal field of view (CameraClass::Set_View_Plane)
///   to 50 degrees scaled by the view's share of the display width.
///   That field of view does not last: every time
///   W3DView::setCameraTransform rebuilds the camera, it sets the field of
///   view again from the view's
///   own field-of-view value (the member Zero Hour and the BFME 1
///   reconstruction call m_FOV), or from a debug value in the global data
///   when a debug-camera flag there is set, and in one camera mode it sets a
///   second, mode-specific value. The field-of-view change made in
///   W3DView::setWidth is overwritten when setCameraTransform next runs.
///   Retail also calls CameraClass::Set_Viewport,
///   CameraClass::Set_Aspect_Ratio and CameraClass::Set_View_Plane from
///   other sites whose callers are not identified, so a resolution or
///   aspect change must check those too.
/// - Retail code outside HTreeClass reads the pivot array directly; see the
///   class's Remastering note before changing the pivot layout.


/// \file WW3D2/ww3dformat_bits.cpp
/// \ingroup grp_w2_ww3d_core_helpers
/// \brief Get_Bits_Per_Pixel, the texture format bit-depth table.
///
/// Ported from the Open-BFME-1 unit of the same name. The unit declares its
/// own WW3DFormat enumeration with Direct3D's D3DFORMAT values, which are
/// the format values BFME 2 passes; Zero Hour's WW3DFormat numbering is
/// different.


/// \file WW3D2/Log_DX8_ErrorCode.cpp
/// \ingroup grp_w2_ww3d_core_helpers
/// \brief Log_DX8_ErrorCode, BFME 2's fatal handler for failed Direct3D
///        calls.
///
/// A dedicated unit. Besides Log_DX8_ErrorCode it holds the routine that
/// stores tagged data blocks, and declares the part of the debug library's
/// crash interface the handler calls.


/// \file WW3D2/Register_Aggregate_Prototype.cpp
/// \ingroup grp_w2_ww3d_core_helpers
/// \brief Register_Aggregate_Prototype, one of the asset catalog's
///        per-type registrars.
///
/// Ported from the Open-BFME-1 unit of the same name.


/// \file WW3D2/W3d_Shader_Reset.cpp
/// \ingroup grp_w2_ww3d_core_helpers
/// \brief The out-of-line copy of the w3d_file.h inline W3d_Shader_Reset.
///
/// The unit carries its own copy of W3dShaderStruct and the inline, so that
/// it emits the one copy the ledger assigns to it.


/// \class HTreeClass
/// \ingroup grp_w2_ww3d_core_helpers
/// \brief A hierarchy tree: the named skeleton of pivots (bones) that an
///        animated W3D model is posed with.
///
/// An HTreeClass holds a name, a pivot count and an array of pivots. Each
/// pivot has a name, a parent pivot, a base (rest-pose) transform relative
/// to its parent, the transform computed for the current frame, an index
/// and a visibility flag. The header describes animation data as changes
/// from the tree's base pose. The tree's update functions, Base_Update,
/// Anim_Update, Blend_Update and Combo_Update, are declared in the BFME 1
/// header; what this page says about them comes from Zero Hour, not from
/// BFME 2's bodies. Zero Hour: they turn a root transform plus animation
/// frames into one transform per pivot, and the sub-objects of a
/// hierarchical model are placed at the transforms of the bones they are
/// attached to.
///
/// \par Ownership and lifetime
/// Source trees are loaded from the hierarchy chunks of a W3D file by
/// HTreeClass::Load_W3D. The render objects that animate a tree work on
/// their own copy, which they then own:
/// - Animatable3DObjClass's name constructor copies the named source tree
///   with the copy constructor. When the hierarchy name is empty, or names
///   no hierarchy that can be found, it creates a new tree and calls
///   HTreeClass::Init_Default. Given a null name it creates no tree at all,
///   and the object has no hierarchy.
/// - HLodClass's LOD-array constructor always creates one: a copy of its
///   highest LOD's tree, or a default tree from HTreeClass::Init_Default
///   when that LOD has none.
///
/// Animatable3DObjClass's destructor and assignment operator destroy the
/// tree the object owns, if any, which runs HTreeClass::~HTreeClass; the
/// assignment operator then copies the other object's tree. The hierarchy
/// prototype code destroys its trees the same way.
///
/// \par BFME 2 layout
/// Established from the BFME 2 bodies:
/// - Each pivot stores its base and current transforms as a rotation
///   quaternion followed by a translation, where Zero Hour stores 3x4
///   matrices. HTreeClass::Init_Default makes both identity.
///   HTreeClass::read_pivots fills the base transform from the file and
///   sets only the root pivot's current transform, to identity. Zero
///   Hour computes the other pivots' current transforms in the tree's
///   update functions.
/// - Each pivot carries one extra float after its visibility flag. The
///   BFME 1 reconstruction names it PivotFade. There, Base_Update resets
///   it to 1.0, and Anim_Update stores a per-pivot fade value that it
///   reads from the animation (Zero Hour has neither the field nor the
///   call). In BFME 2, HTreeClass::Init_Default and HTreeClass::read_pivots
///   set it to 1.0 on the root pivot; whether BFME 2's animation update
///   fills it the same way is not established.
/// - The tree object is larger than Zero Hour's. After the scale factor it
///   holds a sorted list of captured-bone records, one per captured pivot:
///   the pivot index, a rotation, a translation and a world-space flag.
///   HTreeClass::Capture_Bone, HTreeClass::Control_Bone,
///   HTreeClass::Release_Bone and HTreeClass::Is_Bone_Captured use it in
///   place of Zero Hour's per-pivot capture fields. The tree also owns a
///   second buffer, released by HTreeClass::Free, whose role is unknown.
///
/// \par Invariants
/// - HTreeClass::Free releases the pivot array, sets the pivot count to 0
///   and the scale factor to 1.0, empties the captured-bone list without
///   releasing its storage, and releases the second buffer. Load_W3D and
///   Init_Default call it first, so both can be called on a tree that
///   already holds pivots.
/// - Pivot 0 is the root and has no parent. HTreeClass::Load_W3D inserts
///   a root pivot into hierarchy files older than format version 3.0,
///   which lacked one.
///
/// \par Remastering
/// The pivot record's size and member order are fixed by code outside the
/// class that indexes the pivot array directly. In BFME 2,
/// Animatable3DObjClass::Get_Bone_Transform and
/// Animatable3DObjClass::Simple_Evaluate_Bone read a pivot's rotation and
/// translation straight from the tree's array. Retail also holds an
/// unreferenced out-of-line copy of the pivot transform accessor (its name,
/// HTreeClass::Get_Transform, is a structural inference). Changing the
/// pivot layout means rebuilding every unit that reads pivots, not only
/// this class.
///
/// \see HTreeClass::Init_Default, HTreeClass::~HTreeClass,
///      HTreeClass::Load_W3D, HTreeClass::Get_Bone_Index


/// \fn void HTreeClass::Init_Default(void)
/// \ingroup grp_w2_ww3d_core_helpers
/// \brief Turns the tree into the default skeleton: one root pivot named
///        "RootTransform" at the identity transform.
///
/// The fallback for models without a usable hierarchy. In BFME 2,
/// Animatable3DObjClass's name constructor calls it when the hierarchy name
/// is empty or the named hierarchy is not found, and HLodClass's LOD-array
/// constructor calls it when its highest LOD has no hierarchy.
///
/// \par Implementation
/// - Calls HTreeClass::Free first, so any previous pivots and captured
///   bones are discarded.
/// - Allocates a one-element pivot array with the array form of operator
///   new, which runs the pivot constructor.
/// - Sets the pivot's index to 0 and its parent to none, makes its base and
///   current transforms identity (rotation quaternion (0, 0, 0, 1), zero
///   translation), marks it visible, sets its extra per-pivot scalar to 1.0
///   and names it "RootTransform".
/// - Clears the tree's own name to the empty string.
///
/// Apart from the extra scalar and the pivot's transform format, this is
/// Zero Hour's Init_Default.
///
/// \see HTreeClass::Free, HTreeClass::Load_W3D, HTreeClass::~HTreeClass


/// \fn HTreeClass::~HTreeClass(void)
/// \ingroup grp_w2_ww3d_core_helpers
/// \brief Releases everything the tree owns.
///
/// Runs when an Animatable3DObjClass (or an HLodClass, which derives from
/// it) is destroyed or reassigned, and when the hierarchy prototype code
/// releases a source tree, which it does by calling the destructor
/// directly and then freeing the memory.
///
/// \par Implementation
/// Calls HTreeClass::Free, which releases the pivot array and the second
/// buffer and empties the captured-bone list. Free leaves the list's
/// storage allocated, so the destructor then releases that storage itself,
/// through free. In this game free is the memory manager's replacement,
/// which returns the block to the game allocator; BFME 2's STL containers
/// release their storage the same way. Zero Hour's destructor only calls
/// Free, because its tree has no such list.
///
/// \see HTreeClass::Free, HTreeClass::Init_Default


/// \fn unsigned Get_Bits_Per_Pixel(WW3DFormat format)
/// \brief Returns how many bits one pixel of a texture format occupies, or 0
///        for a format the table does not know.
///
/// BFME 2's texture-memory accounting is built on it. The one identified
/// estimate multiplies the bit depth by the texture's three dimensions and
/// divides by eight to get bytes, then scales the result up for six-faced
/// textures (apparently cube maps) and for textures with a mipmap chain,
/// and adds a fixed per-surface amount. A texture-memory-usage query on
/// texture references also calls it.
///
/// \par Implementation
/// A switch on the format:
/// - 32 bits: A8R8G8B8 and X8R8G8B8.
/// - 24 bits: R8G8B8.
/// - 16 bits: R5G6B5, X1R5G5B5, A1R5G5B5, A4R4G4B4, A8R3G3B2, X4R4G4B4, A8P8
///   and A8L8.
/// - 8 bits: R3G3B2, A8, P8, L8, A4L4 and the compressed DXT2 to DXT5.
/// - 4 bits: the compressed DXT1.
/// - 0: anything else, including the bump-map formats.
///
/// The case values are Direct3D's D3DFORMAT codes, including the
/// four-character codes 'DXT1' to 'DXT5'; the unit declares its own
/// WW3DFormat enumeration with those values. Zero Hour's WW3DFormat is a
/// dense enumeration numbered from zero, so code that passes Zero
/// Hour-numbered WW3DFormat constants to this function gets wrong results.
/// Zero Hour's nearest relative is Get_Bytes_Per_Pixel, which cannot
/// express DXT1's half a byte per pixel. Retail passes the format in a
/// register (__fastcall).
///
/// \param format A texture format, as a D3DFORMAT code.
/// \return Bits per pixel: 32, 24, 16, 8 or 4, or 0 for an unlisted format.
///
/// \note The name comes from the Open-BFME-1 reconstruction. Zero Hour has
///       no function of this name, and the reconstruction cites no retail
///       symbol for it.
///
/// \par Remastering
/// The table covers only the formats the original renderer used. A port
/// that adds formats (BC7 or floating-point textures, for example) must
/// extend it, or their memory will be counted as zero.


/// \fn void Log_DX8_ErrorCode(unsigned res)
/// \brief Reports a failed Direct3D call as a fatal crash, recording the
///        error code for the crash dump.
///
/// The out-of-line half of DX8_ErrorCode, the inline check in dx8wrapper.h
/// that returns at once when a Direct3D result is D3D_OK and otherwise
/// calls this function. BFME 2 keeps an out-of-line copy of DX8_ErrorCode
/// as well. Callers include texture surface operations
/// (SurfaceClass::Get_Description, SurfaceClass::DrawPixel), the vertex and
/// index buffer lock classes, DX8Wrapper::End_Scene, WW3D::Make_Screen_Shot,
/// WW3D::Update_Movie_Capture, terrain texture updates
/// (TerrainTextureClass::updateFlatDXT1, TerrainTextureClass::updateCliff)
/// and W3DAptRenderer::BeginTriangleList.
///
/// \par Implementation
/// - Saves a copy of the error code under the four-character tag 'DXER'
///   in a table of tagged data blocks, which the reconstruction treats as
///   crash-dump data.
/// - Raises a crash through the EA debug library in the shape of Zero
///   Hour's DCRASH_RELEASE macro: Debug::SkipNext, then Debug::CrashBegin
///   with no file or line, the text "Direct3D error " followed by the
///   numeric code, and Debug::CrashDone in its fatal mode. BFME 2's
///   Debug::CrashDone takes a mode where Zero Hour's takes an exit flag:
///   report, fatal, or append the report to errors.txt.
///
/// Zero Hour's version instead looks the code up with D3DXGetErrorStringA,
/// prints the description to the debug log and asserts; a release build
/// compiles out both the message and the assertion. BFME 2's retail build
/// therefore stops on errors that Zero Hour's release build passed over.
///
/// \param res The Direct3D result (HRESULT) that was not D3D_OK.
///
/// \warning By default this function does not return. In its fatal mode,
///          BFME 2's Debug::CrashDone adds a line saying the game will now
///          exit to the crash report, shows the report in a "Game crash"
///          message box, then raises an exception and ends the process.
///          Debug::CrashDone only does this when Debug::CrashBegin started
///          a report. If the debug library is set to skip this crash site,
///          no report is made and the function returns.
///
/// \par Remastering
/// This is the renderer's error policy for checked Direct3D calls. A
/// backend that can lose its device, or fail to create a resource under
/// memory pressure, needs a recoverable path here and in DX8_ErrorCode
/// instead of a crash.
/// \see DX8Wrapper::End_Scene


/// \fn void Register_Aggregate_Prototype(const char *name, int first, int second)
/// \brief Registers one entry of the asset catalog as a prototype under its
///        name, unless an asset of that name is already registered.
///
/// BFME 2 reads a binary asset catalog (asset.dat, according to the
/// WorldBuilder build's error messages). For each asset it lists the name,
/// a four-character type tag and two 32-bit values, and the catalog reader
/// calls a registrar chosen by the tag. This function is the registrar the
/// reader calls for entries tagged 'ANIM'.
///
/// \par Implementation
/// When name is not null and Render_Obj_Exists reports no asset of that
/// name, it allocates a prototype object that stores the name and the two
/// values unchanged, and passes it to Add_Prototype, which adds it to the
/// asset registry. Otherwise it does nothing, so the first registration of
/// a name wins.
///
/// \param name   Asset name. The catalog reader lower-cases it first. Null
///               is ignored.
/// \param first  First 32-bit value from the catalog entry, stored in the
///               prototype. Open-BFME-1 reads the pair as the asset's
///               offset and length.
/// \param second Second 32-bit value from the catalog entry, stored in the
///               prototype.
///
/// \warning Name not settled. The name comes from the Open-BFME-1 donor,
///          whose catalog reader calls the same-shaped registrar for
///          aggregate ('AGGR') entries. In BFME 2 the retail reader calls
///          this function for animation ('ANIM') entries and sends 'AGGR'
///          entries to a different registrar. For 'ANIM' entries, the
///          BFME 1 donor's catalog reader calls
///          Register_Animation_Prototype, and the WorldBuilder build's
///          reader calls a function that the WorldBuilder build names
///          AnimationAsset::Register (no WorldBuilder function is paired
///          with this BFME 2 body). So this is probably the animation
///          registrar; neither name is confirmed for BFME 2. The
///          prototype class it builds is not established either, and the
///          name may change.
///
/// \par Modding
/// Registration is by name only. An entry whose name is already registered
/// is skipped here, and the catalog reader also skips it before calling,
/// so the first registration of a name wins.
/// \see Render_Obj_Exists, Add_Prototype


/// \fn int MeshModelClass::Compute_Ram_Size(void)
/// \ingroup grp_w2_ww3d_core_helpers
/// \brief Estimates the memory a mesh model's shared data occupies, in
///        bytes.
///
/// The sum of the geometry's own estimate, MeshGeometryClass's
/// Compute_Ram_Size, and the estimates of the material data the model owns.
/// Its one identified BFME 2 caller is an accessor, of a class whose
/// identity is not established, that returns a fixed base size, plus this
/// estimate when it holds a mesh model. What
/// consumes that total, for example a memory budget or a statistics
/// report, is not established. Zero Hour has no
/// MeshModelClass::Compute_Ram_Size; its only Compute_Ram_Size is
/// AABTreeClass's inline in aabtree.h. The mesh model, geometry and
/// material-description estimates come from the BFME 1 reconstruction.
///
/// \par Implementation
/// Adds, for each one that is present:
/// - the default material description's MeshMatDescClass estimate;
/// - the alternate material description's estimate;
/// - the material information's estimate (the member Zero Hour and BFME 1
///   declare as a MaterialInfoClass pointer; BFME 2's type for it is not
///   confirmed).
///
/// The current material description is not counted on its own. In Zero
/// Hour it always points at the default or the alternate one, so counting
/// it would count that description twice.
///
/// \return The estimate in bytes. In the BFME 1 reconstruction, the parts
///         it adds up are computed from element counts and fixed sizes,
///         not measured from the allocations.


/// \fn void MeshMatDescClass::Disable_Backface_Culling(void)
/// \ingroup grp_w2_ww3d_core_helpers
/// \brief Turns off back-face culling in every pass of a mesh's materials,
///        so that both sides of each triangle are drawn.
///
/// Called by MeshModelClass::post_process, for the default and (when
/// present) the alternate material description, when the mesh's TWO_SIDED
/// flag is set. BFME 2's MeshModelClass::Load_W3D sets that flag from the
/// W3D_MESH_FLAG_TWO_SIDED bit in the W3D mesh header and calls
/// post_process at the end of loading, as Zero Hour's does.
///
/// \par Implementation
/// For each pass up to the material description's pass count, clears the
/// cull-mode flag of the pass's shader, and, if the pass has a per-triangle
/// shader array, the same flag of every shader in it. Clearing the flag
/// selects CULL_MODE_DISABLE.
///
/// \note The retail body clears a different bit of the packed shader word
///       than Zero Hour's ShaderClass::Set_Cull_Mode would, so BFME 2's
///       shader bit layout differs from Zero Hour's at least here. The
///       unit therefore uses its own view of the class. Check the bit
///       layout against retail before reusing Zero Hour's shader masks.
///
/// \par Modding
/// Two-sidedness is a per-mesh attribute in the W3D file's mesh header, not
/// an INI setting: BFME 2's MeshModelClass::Load_W3D reads it from there,
/// as Zero Hour's does.


/// \fn SegLineRendererClass &SegLineRendererClass::operator=(const SegLineRendererClass &that)
/// \ingroup grp_w2_ww3d_core_helpers
/// \brief Copies another line renderer's texture, shader and settings, but
///        not its vertex buffer.
///
/// SegLineRendererClass is the low-level line renderer that, as its header
/// describes it, both SegmentedLineClass and the particle buffers use. Its
/// copy constructor runs this operator, and the assignment operators of
/// SegmentedLineClass and StreakLineClass use it to copy their renderer.
///
/// \par Implementation
/// Assigning an object to itself does nothing. Otherwise:
/// - the texture is shared by reference count: the incoming texture gains
///   a reference before the old one is released, so assigning the same
///   texture is safe;
/// - the shader, width, colour, opacity, subdivision level, noise
///   amplitude, merge-abort factor, texture tile factor, last-used sync
///   time, UV offset, UV offset rate and flag bits are copied;
/// - BFME 2's extra field, whose role is unknown, is copied as well;
/// - the vertex buffer and its size stay with the destination, as in Zero
///   Hour.
///
/// \param that The renderer to copy from.
/// \return This renderer.


/// \fn void W3d_Shader_Reset(W3dShaderStruct *s)
/// \brief Fills a W3D file shader description with the format's default
///        settings.
///
/// W3dShaderStruct is the shader as stored in a W3D file. The defaults are
/// depth test "pass if less or equal" with depth writes on, destination
/// blend zero and source blend one (opaque), primary gradient modulate,
/// and everything else disabled or zero, including the fields the format
/// marks obsolete. These are the W3DSHADER_..._DEFAULT values of
/// w3d_file.h.
///
/// In BFME 2 it has one identified caller, W3dUtilityClass::Convert_Shader
/// (the ShaderClass to W3dShaderStruct direction), which resets the
/// structure and then copies the live shader's settings in. The particle
/// emitter definition reaches that conversion through
/// ParticleEmitterDefClass::Set_Shader,
/// ParticleEmitterDefClass::Convert_To_Ver2 and
/// ParticleEmitterDefClass::Initialize_To_Ver2.
///
/// \par Implementation
/// The inline from w3d_file.h, emitted out of line. It writes every byte
/// of the structure, including its padding byte.
///
/// \param s [out] The structure to fill. Must not be null.


/// \fn void CameraClass::Set_Viewport(const Vector2 &min, const Vector2 &max)
/// \ingroup grp_w2_ww3d_core_helpers
/// \brief Sets the rectangle of the screen the camera renders into, as
///        fractions of the display.
///
/// The viewport's corners are normalised coordinates: (0, 0) is the
/// top-left of the display and (1, 1) the bottom-right. In Zero Hour a new
/// camera's viewport is the whole display. Its identified BFME 2 callers
/// include W3DView::setOrigin, W3DView::setWidth and W3DView::setHeight;
/// retail also calls it from other sites whose callers are not identified
/// here. setOrigin converts the view's pixel origin, and setWidth and
/// setHeight its right and bottom edge (origin plus width or height), to
/// fractions of the display size. setOrigin then calls setWidth and
/// setHeight, so the far corner follows the moved origin. Zero Hour:
/// CameraClass::Apply multiplies the fractions by the render target's
/// resolution when it builds the Direct3D viewport.
///
/// \par Implementation
/// The inline from camera.h, emitted out of line in BFME 2. It stores both
/// corners and marks the camera's cached frustum invalid, so the frustum
/// is rebuilt the next time it is needed.
///
/// \param min Top-left corner, as fractions of the display width and
///            height.
/// \param max Bottom-right corner, as fractions of the display width and
///            height.
///
/// \par Remastering
/// Because the viewport is relative, a resolution change needs no change
/// here. The aspect ratio and field of view are separate camera settings.
/// Among identified callers, W3DView::setWidth and W3DView::setHeight set
/// a W3DView camera's aspect ratio (CameraClass::Set_Aspect_Ratio).
/// W3DView::setWidth also sets its field of view
/// (CameraClass::Set_View_Plane), and W3DView::setCameraTransform sets it
/// again each time it rebuilds the camera (see the group's Remastering
/// note). Retail also calls this function, CameraClass::Set_Aspect_Ratio
/// and CameraClass::Set_View_Plane from other functions whose identity is
/// not established, so check those as well before changing resolution or
/// aspect handling.
